s: Reduces the impact of outliers in this feature while preserving its relative order and potential importance in interactions with condensation reactions.\ndf['sqrt_F03C-O'] = np.sqrt(df['F03[C-O]'])\n\n# SpMax_B_cube\n# Cube transformation of the leading eigenvalue from Burden matrix weighted by mass (SpMax_B(m)).\n# Usefulness: Highlights extreme values of the structural feature for specific edge cases that may drive biodegradability.\ndf['SpMax_B_cube'] = df['SpMax_B(m)']  3\nend  \n\n### Step 3: Statistical Encoding and Dimensionality Reduction\nReduce noise or sparsity in features by encoding or mixing features into aggregate statistical representations.\n\npython\n# nC_H_ratio\n# Ratio of number of heavy atoms (nHM) to the number of Hydrogen donor atoms (nHDon).\n# Usefulness: Captures the balance of molecular density versus hydrogen donors affecting biodegradation likelihood.\ndf['nC_H_ratio'] = df['nHM'] / (df['nHDon'] + 1e-10)\n\n# zscore_SdO\n# Z-score normalization of the sum of dO E-states (SdO).\n# Usefulness: Centers and scales the feature, which has a wide range and could dominate other features unfairly in the model.\ndf['zscore_SdO'] = (df['SdO'] - df['SdO'].mean()) / (df['SdO'].std() + 1e-10)\n\n# SdssC_F04C-N_rolling_mean\n# Rolling mean of the sum of dssC E-states (SdssC) and frequency of C-N bonds at topological distance 4 (F04[C-N]).\n# Usefulness: Captures short-term trends or temporal pairing effects in closely related molecular components that might indicate biodegradability.\ndf['SdssC_F04C-N_rolling_mean'] = (df['SdssC'] + df['F04[C-N]']).rolling(window=3, min_periods=1).mean()\nend  \n\n### Dropped Columns\nSome features may be irrelevant or redundant due to low variability or poor utilization in the model. For example:\npython\n# Explanation why the column nCrt is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['nCrt'], inplace=True)\n# nCrt (number of ring tertiary C(sp3)) has consistently low values (mean = 0.139, median = 0.0) across the dataset, providing minimal variability for the model to learn from.\n\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n# This feature (presence of C-Br at a short distance) has a near-zero mean and low frequency in the dataset, making it unlikely to contribute significantly to the model.\n\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\n# N-073 (specific nitrogen types) has low variance (mean = 0.025, median = 0.0) and adds minimal value due to its sparsity and limited distribution.\nend", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering, along with explanations for dropping irrelevant columns:\n\n### Step 1: Interaction Features\nCombine certain attribute pairs to explore their potential interactions, which could have a more significant impact on predicting biodegradability.\n\npython\n# nN_C_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of the number of Nitrogen atoms (nN) to the percentage of Carbon atoms (C%).\n# Usefulness: Captures the relative abundance of Nitrogen compared to Carbon, which may influence chemical reactivity and biodegradation pathways.\ndf['nN_C_ratio'] = df['nN'] / (df['C%'] + 1e-10)\nend\n\n### Step 2: Non-linear Transformation\nApply a non-linear transformation to a highly variable feature to capture diminishing effects or non-linear patterns.\n\npython\n# sqrt_SdssC\n# Feature: Square root of the sum of dssC E-states.\n# Usefulness: Reduces the impact of outliers and highlights smaller values, potentially capturing subtle patterns in molecular reactivity affecting biodegradability.\ndf['sqrt_SdssC'] = np.sqrt(df['SdssC'].clip(lower=0))\nend\n\n### Step 3: Ratio-Based Feature\nCreate a new ratio feature to evaluate the relationship between heavy atoms and primary carbons.\n\npython\n# nHM_to_nCp_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of the number of heavy atoms (nHM) to the number of terminal primary C(sp3) atoms (nCp).\n# Usefulness: Highlights the structural density