al impact on biodegradability.\ndf['nCb_minus_to_C_ratio'] = df['nCb-'] / (df['C%'] + 0.001)  # Adding 0.001 to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Non-linear Transformation - Log Transformation of SM6_B(m)\npython\n# log_SM6_B_m\n# Feature description: Applies logarithmic transformation to the spectral moment of order 6 from the Burden matrix weighted by mass (SM6_B(m)).\n# Usefulness: Helps capture non-linear relationships by mitigating the influence of large values while highlighting smaller but meaningful variations in molecular topology.\ndf['log_SM6_B_m'] = np.log1p(df['SM6_B(m)'])  # Adding 1 to safely handle zero values.\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Combined Feature - Ratio of nCp to Sum of nCp and nHM\npython\n# nCp_to_nCp_plus_nHM\n# Feature description: This ratio compares the number of terminal primary C(sp3) atoms (nCp) to the sum of nCp and the number of heavy atoms (nHM).\n# Usefulness: Emphasizes the proportion of reactive primary carbons relative to the molecule's overall structural density, which could influence biodegradability.\ndf['nCp_to_nCp_plus_nHM'] = df['nCp'] / (df['nCp'] + df['nHM'] + 1e-10)  # Adding 1e-10 to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropped Columns\n\n#### Column 1: B04[C-Br]\npython\n# Explanation why the column B04[C-Br] is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['B04[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n# B04[C-Br] is a binary feature with nearly all values set to 0 (mean = 0.0332, standard deviation = 0.1792). Its extremely low variance makes it uninformative for predictive modeling.\nend\n\n#### Column 2: nN-N\npython\n# Explanation why the column nN-N is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['nN-N'], inplace=True)\n# nN-N (number of N hydrazines) has extremely sparse data (mean = 0.0111) with no meaningful variability (median = Q3 = 0). It is unlikely to contribute to model performance.\nend\n\n#### Column 3: N-073\npython\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\n# N-073 (specific nitrogen types) has very low values (mean = 0.0253, median = Q3 = 0), providing almost no information to distinguish between classes.\nend", "### Feature Engineering Steps\n\nHere are three feature engineering steps to improve predictions for classifying chemicals as ready or not ready biodegradable:\n\npython\n# Step 1: Interaction Feature - Product of SpMax_L and J_Dz(e)\n# Feature: SpMax_L_J_Dz_product\n# Feature description: Product of the leading eigenvalue from the Laplace matrix (SpMax_L) and the Balaban-like index from the Barysz matrix weighted by Sanderson electronegativity (J_Dz(e)).\n# Usefulness: Combines two topological molecular descriptors that relate to structural and electronegativity properties, providing nonlinear interaction effects that may influence biodegradability.\ndf['SpMax_L_J_Dz_product'] = df['SpMax_L'] * df['J_Dz(e)']\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Non-linear Transformation - Log transformation of nCIR\n# Feature: log_nCIR\n# Feature description: Logarithmic transformation of the number of circuits (nCIR).\n# Usefulness: Reduces the skew caused by a high range of circuit values while highlighting fine differences in smaller ranges. This can better capture the role of complex molecular structures in affecting biodegradability.\ndf['log_nCIR'] = np.log1p(df['nCIR'])  # Adding 1 to handle zero values safely.\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Ratio Feature - Ratio of nCp (terminal primary C(sp3)) to nCb- (number of substituted benzene C(sp2))\n# Feature: nCp_to_nCb_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of terminal primary C(sp3) atoms (nCp) to substituted benzene C(sp2) atoms (nCb-).\n# Usefulness: Highlights the balance between aliphatic (simpler) and aromatic (more complex) molecular structures, which may influence a molecule's readiness for biodegradation.\ndf['nCp_to_nCb_ratio'] = df['nCp'] / (df['nCb-'] + 1e-10)  # Adding small value to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns\n\nRemove features with low variance or redundancy in the da